* current-limit -- constant-current (brickwall) limit characterization for
* ldo_core with Xilimit (issue #11).
*
* Substantiates the ratified "Current limit" row of README.md's target
* specification: threshold over PVT, constant-current (not folded-back)
* behaviour, and -- the safety-critical half -- that the limit never engages
* for I_load in [0, 50] mA at any corner.
*
* Two load styles share one deck, selected by `alter` from the .control
* block below, because both claims must be taken at the SAME PVT point:
*
*   regulation mode   Rforce = 1 Gohm (the forcing path is effectively
*                     absent), Rload is a resistive load. Answers "is the
*                     output still in the +/-2% window at 0 mA and at
*                     50 mA?", i.e. did the limit engage inside the rated
*                     load range.
*   V-I mode          Rforce = 1 mohm (the forcing source owns VOUT),
*                     Rload open. Sweeping the forced VOUT down to a dead
*                     short traces the whole current-limit characteristic;
*                     its FLATNESS is the brickwall claim, and its value is
*                     the threshold.
*
* Both load styles are RESISTIVE or VOLTAGE-FORCED, never an ideal current
* sink, and that is deliberate. With an ideal current sink the only DC paths
* left at VOUT are the feedback divider (300 kohm) and the sense/threshold
* resistors, so the node's DC impedance is enormous and the operating-point
* solve is badly conditioned: sweeping a current sink from 0 to 55 mA on
* this circuit returns physically absurd answers (Vout = -64 V at 2 mA,
* +1.802 V at 6 mA, -3.5 V at 15 mA) that are solver artifacts, not a
* bistable circuit. A resistor cannot pull the output below ground and
* keeps the node's impedance bounded, so every point converges to the
* physical branch. The load current is measured (Vlmeas) rather than
* assumed, since a resistive load's current depends on the settled Vout.
*
* 1 mohm at 90 mA is a 90 uV error on the forced node and 1 Gohm at 1.8 V
* leaks 1.8 nA: both are far below anything measured here.
*
* ERRAMP_OUT and PASS_GATE are ldo_core's deliberately separate loop-break
* ports; normal operation ties them together at instantiation (net LOOP),
* exactly as sim/op-point-sanity does. See design/README.md.
*
* Template: run.sh substitutes the @TOKENS@ (PDK paths, corner sections,
* temperature, supply) and runs the result with `ngspice -b`.

.include "@DESIGN_INCLUDE@"
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @MOS_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @RES_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @BJT_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @DIODE_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @MOSCAP_CORNER@
.lib "@MODEL_LIB@" @MIMCAP_CORNER@

.temp @TEMP_C@

.include "@LDO_NETLIST@"

* --- stimulus ------------------------------------------------------------
Vin VIN 0 DC @VIN_V@
Ven EN 0 DC @VIN_V@

* --- DUT -----------------------------------------------------------------
* Port order (design/ldo_core.sym / .sch): VIN VOUT EN VSS ERRAMP_OUT PASS_GATE
Xdut VIN VOUT EN 0 LOOP LOOP ldo_core

* --- load ----------------------------------------------------------------
* Rload values: 1 Gohm = open, 72 ohm ~ 25 mA, 36 ohm ~ 50 mA at Vout = 1.8 V.
Rload VOUT LN 1G
Vlmeas LN 0 DC 0
Vforce VF 0 DC 0
Rforce VOUT VF 1G

* Each `op` below starts a NEW ngspice plot, and `let` vectors live in the
* plot that was current when they were created -- so every measurement is
* printed immediately after the analysis that produced it, and all derived
* quantities (limit spread, brickwall flatness, short-circuit power) are
* computed by the rollup in run.sh rather than across plots here.
.control
set noaskquit

* ======================================================================
* Regulation mode: does the limit engage anywhere inside 0..50 mA?
* ======================================================================
alter Rforce = 1G

alter Rload = 1G
op
let m_vout_0ma = v(vout)
let m_ivin_0ma_ua = -i(vin)*1e6
print m_vout_0ma
print m_ivin_0ma_ua

alter Rload = 72
op
let m_vout_25ma = v(vout)
let m_iload_25ma = i(vlmeas)*1e3
print m_vout_25ma
print m_iload_25ma

alter Rload = 36
op
let m_vout_50ma = v(vout)
let m_iload_50ma = i(vlmeas)*1e3
let m_clg_50ma = v(xdut.xilimit.clg)
let m_isns_mv_50ma = (v(xdut.xilimit.isns) - v(vout))*1e3
let m_vth_mv_50ma = (v(xdut.xilimit.vth) - v(vout))*1e3
* Sense-path headroom at the rated load: how far below the threshold the
* sensed voltage still is. This is the margin the limit has against
* nuisance-tripping inside the rated range, expressed in the sense domain.
let m_sense_margin_pct = (m_vth_mv_50ma - m_isns_mv_50ma)/m_vth_mv_50ma*100
print m_vout_50ma
print m_clg_50ma
print m_isns_mv_50ma
print m_vth_mv_50ma
print m_sense_margin_pct
print m_iload_50ma

alter Rload = 1G

* ======================================================================
* V-I mode: the current-limit characteristic
* ======================================================================
alter Rforce = 1m

alter Vforce = 1.764
op
let m_ilim_1764_ma = i(vforce)*1e3
print m_ilim_1764_ma

alter Vforce = 1.5
op
let m_ilim_1500_ma = i(vforce)*1e3
print m_ilim_1500_ma

alter Vforce = 0.9
op
let m_ilim_0900_ma = i(vforce)*1e3
print m_ilim_0900_ma

alter Vforce = 0.3
op
let m_ilim_0300_ma = i(vforce)*1e3
print m_ilim_0300_ma

alter Vforce = 0
op
let m_ilim_0000_ma = i(vforce)*1e3
let m_pshort_mw = m_ilim_0000_ma*@VIN_V@
print m_ilim_0000_ma
print m_pshort_mw

* Full characteristic, for the record's raw evidence.
dc Vforce 0 1.9 0.05
let vi_ma = i(vforce)*1e3
print v(vf) vi_ma
.endc
.end
